my identity has always been a rule  follower and now with edith i feel like  we're  breaking every rule  [Music]  people think of jewish food either as  israeli or middle eastern food or the  classic jewish deli  and there's kind of this big space in  between the two where a lot gets lost  there were jewish people that lived in  morocco and mexico so naturally when  people go to different regions or  different places they'll pick up  different techniques and products and  cultures and ingredients and it's kind  of like this amazing blend  being born out of displacement led to  all these different cultural experiences  and that's where edith comes into play  you really try to explore where these  untold stories come from and try to tell  them in a really approachable way  through our food just like they say  they're going to go out for thai food or  chinese food or indian food or mexican  food my goal is with edis is for people  to be like i'm going to go out for  jewish food tonight  my name's alyssa and i'm the founder of  edith's in brooklyn  we're sitting in our little teeny tiny  sandwich shop that we just opened this  past march  everybody knows a sandwich it's just  about how playful how creative we can  get with it while being true to like the  region we have a sandwich that's called  the maghreb which is focused on the  maghreb region of like northern africa  we have another sandwich called the  alhira made with a harissa mayo and like  this portuguese style chicken sausage  when the spanish inquisition was  happening and jews were like forced out  of spain they fled to portugal  jewish butchers never butchered pork  because  it's not kosher and a lot of sausage  especially in that region is all pork  based  so to help jewish people hide especially  as butchers they taught them how to make  an al hira style sausage but with  chicken or like meat that is kosher and  when i found that out i was like this is  an amazing story that we can kind of  tell through food  [Music]  the bagel here  is hand twisted because as far as we can  trace history back that was kind of like  the original bagel from krakow in poland  so i thought the twisting would actually  be a more interesting way to eat the  bagel when you twist it you get these  nooks and crannies and you get some  parts of the bagel that are a little bit  like baked more  some that are a little softer  i didn't realize how hard it would be to  sell a non-new york style bagel  in new york people have a very certain  idea what they're looking for when they  order a bagel and doing something  different than that has been  really  empowering there can be more  interpretations of a bagel and part of  a bagel is that it is boiled the reason  for boiling bagels came from the fact  that jews they weren't allowed to bake  bread so they boiled it there is an  element of creativity and there is an  element of history and storytelling and  trying to keep something true to where  it came from  it was this really serendipitous story  of how it all began but we opened our  pop-up just over a year ago  i had started talking about edith's and  i had already known that i wanted to  name it edith because edith was she's my  great aunt but she was the one person in  my life who i like really had a very  special connection with  i talked to my cousin edith's daughter  i'm  gonna do this pop-up i'm gonna call it  edith here she goes you know my mom  owned a deli and i was like what i had  no idea that she actually did own a deli  and that i  70 or 80 years later was starting this  jewish deli inspired pop-up and wanted  to name it edith  i had to find a place to actually make  everything that's not my apartment and i  emailed 15 pizzerias knowing that they  were mostly closed during the day and i  needed an oven  nobody got back to me except for paulie  and paulie emailed me back right away i  was like oh my god paulie g got back to  me i was like that's amazing i had eaten  his pizza for years i go down to 60  greenpoint and he's sitting in his big  lounge chair  you're technically our first investor i  know what i had here we weren't using it  and i like to help people get off the  ground and i love the idea just because  i'm half jewish  what i found interesting is that it's my  heritage i embrace the opportunity you  know have us serve that food here  because it was part of my heritage  cook what you love  don't come up with something that you  don't like just because you think  somebody else will buy it make that food  an extension of you  and  people embrace it  [Music]  moved to new york fresh-faced didn't  know anybody was like ready to come to  work and learn but the things that  comforted me the little pieces of jewish  food that i grew up with i couldn't  afford to eat them here they're just  really expensive and that's kind of when  i started exploring  like all these other parts of jewish  food  oh okay  oh yeah  brighton beach is one of my most  favorite parts of brooklyn it's where  a lot of russian immigrants live i like  make time to do it because i love being  around here and like exploring there's  smells there's sounds there's things  that are new and you go and english is  not a first language for a lot of people  you can kind of communicate again  through food because it's something that  everybody can understand  little speaking  russian  [Music]  it's kind of like cottage cheese yeah  it's like cottage cheese  i didn't grow up eating cottage cheese  so i couldn't really tell you christina  was like our first chef she is half  japanese and we've initially bonded over  like our mutual love of fish  we're like oh  like japanese people eat a ton of fish  jewish people like love a ton like it's  just you realize how  similar everybody is  this is what makes edith special it's  because everybody brings their own  background their own inspiration and  like these harmonious blends of  tradition and cultures and  exploration kind of just like tie  together  ben  he's one of the life bloods of our team  he  comes up with  so many dishes on our menu this is a  dish we've been working on as a team our  take on a traditional like herring and  cream we wanted to take some of those  old world comforting heavier but like  institutional jewish dishes and make  them a little bit easier for people to  digest when i first was given this dish  i had no idea what it was  so i literally did a quick google search  working with christina and ben we have  become so close as like a little family  but also creatively because we've been  able to like use  each other's cultures to like push this  menu forward and like make it what it is  today  this is our take on a traditional like  russian pancake these are all cheeses  from  the russian supermarket the bazaar  we wanted to kind of explore which  cheese would give us like the best final  product i try to keep it wholly unique  and i think what's worked well for us  and like what is  our edith's kind of like ethos is like  going so far backwards in history that  we can make things relevant again  so go to like the depths of the internet  and order crazy  books so we've been testing out  different batters different cheeses  different hydrations textures and  flavors  after that point if i really need like a  serious dose of inspiration or just like  a break i'm always just on my bike i  ride all over brooklyn and i'll eat i'll  sit i'll walk through aisles of like  specialty stores bakeries i'll hear  people speaking  russian and polish i just need to go and  like immerse myself in the cultures that  i'm really taking inspiration from  i'll live in picklebar  here  all like our bakery items will be here  we're opening a second location which is  really the true  realization i think of my dream which is  an all-day eatery and grocery store i  call it a mini  jewish italy but it's really much more  than that a place where people can  explore jewish food from all over the  world in one place through groceries  through  food through  music  we want to create something that's long  lasting like other iconic new york  institutions like the barney green grass  or the russ and daughters that's what we  have to build this with the mindset of  is like staying relevant and putting in  the infrastructure to make sure we can  be here for the next 10 years  jewish food actually didn't have a ton  of real meaning for me my whole life  until now when i started edits and i  start looking back and trying to learn  more about our food and where it comes  from and what it's born out of  and i think that that is the most  beautiful part of it is that  because it's so global in nature and  because the diaspora is so  wide-ranging across multiple countries  and continents and  all of the food cultures that are very  much the same  you see how similar everybody is and  when you encompass a lot of the diaspora  all in one place  you really can see all those  similarities and it just really  brings people together  [Music]  i like it down here  it makes me like just feel good i guess  i feel like i'm on a break from my life  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  you

